---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can every PDF be compressed to 100KB?', a: 'No. A one or two page document almost always can. Long scanned documents often can’t without becoming unreadable. PDFNU tells you when that happens instead of handing you a blurry file.' },
  { q: 'Why did PDFNU convert my pages to images?', a: 'For very small targets, turning each page into a compressed JPEG is sometimes the only way to fit. Text then can’t be selected, but it stays readable. You’re always warned when this happens.' },
  { q: 'Is the 100KB target exact?', a: 'It’s a maximum. The result is as large as possible without going over 100 × 1024 bytes, so it passes 100KB checks on any portal.' },
];
---
<GuideLayout slug="compress-pdf-to-100kb" faqs={faqs}>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> open <a href="/compress-pdf?target=100">Compress PDF</a>, choose the <strong>100KB</strong> target and compress. For one or two pages that’s nearly always enough. For longer scans, remove unneeded pages first or split the file, because 100KB is a very tight budget.</p>

  <h2>How tight is 100KB?</h2>
  <p>
    100KB is roughly one medium-quality photo. If your PDF has five scanned pages, each page gets about 20KB. That’s enough for a clear grayscale page
    of printed text at screen resolution, but not for a detailed color photo. Setting realistic expectations saves a lot of frustration:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Text-based PDFs</strong> (exported from Word, Google Docs, etc.): usually far below 100KB already, unless they contain pictures.</li>
    <li><strong>1–2 scanned pages:</strong> fit comfortably with good readability.</li>
    <li><strong>3–6 scanned pages:</strong> usually fit, with softer images.</li>
    <li><strong>10+ scanned pages:</strong> often only fit by rasterizing at low resolution, so check readability carefully.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="/compress-pdf?target=100">Compress PDF</a> with the 100KB target already selected.</li>
    <li>Choose your file and press <strong>Compress to under 100KB</strong>.</li>
    <li>Read the result card. Green means you’re under 100KB. An amber note means the target wasn’t reachable at a readable quality, and you’re given the smallest readable version.</li>
    <li>Open the downloaded file and zoom in on small print before uploading.</li>
  </ol>

  <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="guide-article" />

  <h2>Tricks that make 100KB possible</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Crop before you convert.</strong> If your PDF came from phone photos, crop each photo to the paper edge, then rebuild the PDF with <a href="/jpg-to-pdf?limit=100">JPG to PDF</a> using its built-in 100KB limit. Desk and shadows waste bytes.</li>
    <li><strong>Delete pages the portal didn’t ask for.</strong> Instructions pages, blank pages, duplicate copies. Use <a href="/organize-pdf">Organize PDF</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Upload separately.</strong> If the form has multiple upload fields, <a href="/split-pdf">split</a> the PDF and give each part its own 100KB.</li>
    <li><strong>Scan in grayscale.</strong> Color scans of black-and-white documents are wasteful.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What PDFNU does behind the scenes</h2>
  <p>
    The compressor first tries a lossless rewrite. If that’s not enough, it re-compresses images with Ghostscript at decreasing resolutions (about
    150, 110, then 72 dpi). Only if the file still doesn’t fit does it render pages to JPEG and lower quality step by step, never below a readable
    floor of 72 dpi. If the target still can’t be met, it stops there rather than giving you something unreadable. For the theory, see
    <a href="/guides/pdf-compression-explained">PDF compression explained</a>.
  </p>
  <p>All of this runs on your device. Your PDF isn’t uploaded at any point.</p>
</GuideLayout>
